\details{
Use it for a low-rank summary of a numeric matrix: compression, denoising,
or a latent-factor representation. The components are linear combinations
of every input column, so nothing is dropped and the components cannot be
read as individual features; use one of featR's selection functions when
you need to name the features you keep. \code{\link[=fs_pca]{fs_pca()}} covers the same ground
with labeled output and variance-explained figures; \code{fs_svd()} is the
lower-level decomposition.

The exact solver is \code{base::svd()}. The approximate solver is
\code{RSpectra::svds()} (from \pkg{RSpectra}, a Suggests dependency needed only
when that solver runs), and it can only return fewer than \code{min(dim(x))}
singular values. The default \code{n_singular_values = NULL} asks for all
\code{min(dim(x))} of them and so always takes the exact path; request fewer to
make the approximate solver available.

With \code{svd_method = "auto"}, the approximate solver runs only when
\code{n_singular_values < min(dim(x))} and \code{min(dim(x)) > svd_threshold}.
Otherwise the exact solver runs, and a message explains why when a large
matrix ends up on the exact path because all singular values were
requested. \code{svd_method = "approx"} with \code{n_singular_values} equal to
\code{min(dim(x))} is not an error: it falls back to the exact solver with a
message. If \code{RSpectra::svds()} converges on fewer singular values than
requested, the call warns and recomputes them with the exact solver.

Centering and scaling happen before the decomposition, so the returned
values and vectors factorize the transformed matrix. With the default
\code{scale_input = TRUE} they reconstruct \code{scale(x)}, not \code{x}. The column means
and standard deviations are not returned; keep them yourself if you need to
map results back to the original units.
